        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync resource</div>
        <h2>What is a data migration mapping sheet?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">A data migration mapping sheet is a table that records, for every field you move, where it comes from, where it goes in HubSpot, what it means, and how its values change on the way. It also captures matching keys, associations, and test results, so the migration is a documented agreement rather than an improvised upload.</p>
        <p class="intro">Migration quality is decided before the first import. We use this sheet on every migration we run, whether the source is Salesforce, Pipedrive, Zoho, or a spreadsheet. Below is each column, why it exists, and how to fill it in.</p>

        <section id="columns">
          <h2>Which columns should a mapping sheet include?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">At minimum: source object and field, target object and property, business meaning, data type, allowed values, transformation rule, decision (migrate, archive, calculate, exclude), matching key, owner, and test status. Each column answers a question someone will otherwise ask in the middle of cutover.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>What to record</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Source object</td><td>The object in the old system</td><td>Account</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Source field</td><td>API name and label of the field</td><td>Industry__c (Industry)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Target object</td><td>The HubSpot object it lands on</td><td>Company</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Target property</td><td>Internal name of the HubSpot property, new or existing</td><td>industry</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Business meaning</td><td>One sentence on what the field means to the business</td><td>Primary industry used for routing and reporting</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Data type</td><td>Text, number, date, dropdown, multi-select, checkbox</td><td>Dropdown</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Allowed values</td><td>The valid options in the target</td><td>The agreed industry list</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Transformation rule</td><td>How values change on the way</td><td>Map each legacy value to one agreed value</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Migrate, archive, calculate, or exclude</td><td>Migrate</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Matching key</td><td>Whether this field identifies the record on import</td><td>No (domain is the key)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Field owner</td><td>Who decides what this field means and who may edit it</td><td>Sales ops</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Test status</td><td>Result of the sample import check</td><td>Passed on the sample batch</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="map-meaning">
          <h2>How do you map a field correctly?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Map by business meaning, not by label. A source field called "Status" may mean something different from a HubSpot property with the same name. Confirm what the field means, its data type, and its allowed values in both systems, then write the transformation rule before any data moves.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Record source object and field alongside the target object and property.</li>
            <li>Define transformation rules for formats, values, dates, and currencies.</li>
            <li>Mark every field as migrate, archive, calculate, or intentionally exclude.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Fields nobody has filled in for a long time are usually candidates to exclude. Fields that HubSpot can derive, such as rollups and counts, are usually better calculated than imported.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="associations">
          <h2>How do you handle associations and history?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Give each object a matching key, store the source system's record ID on every imported record, and import in dependency order: owners, companies, contacts, deals and tickets, then activities. Associations are rebuilt from the stored IDs, so a record keeps its context even when names or emails change.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Document the matching key for each object, such as email for contacts and domain for companies.</li>
            <li>Map associations, ownership, lifecycle, and activity history explicitly, on their own tab.</li>
            <li>Decide which history is required for daily work and which can be archived outside HubSpot.</li>
            <li>Map old users to HubSpot owners, including people who have left.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="test-cutover">
          <h2>How do you test the mapping before cutover?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Run a sample import, then compare source and target values record by record. Reconcile counts per object, check associations and owners on sample records, and confirm the critical reports return the same numbers. Log each result in the test status column and fix the mapping before the full run.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Run a sample import and compare source-to-target values.</li>
            <li>Reconcile record counts, associations, owners, and critical reports.</li>
            <li>Document rollback, freeze windows, and post-cutover verification steps in a cutover log.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The full sequence, from audit to training, is in our <a href="/resources/salesforce-to-hubspot-migration-checklist/">Salesforce to HubSpot migration checklist</a>. The same steps apply to other source systems.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="checklist">
          <h2>Mapping sheet checklist</h2>
          <p>Use these checkpoints to turn the template into a working process.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Source and target fields listed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Business meaning written for each field</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Transformations documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Matching keys confirmed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Associations tested</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Sample import reconciled</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Field owners named</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Cutover and rollback planned</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> A mapping sheet turns migration from a technical upload into a shared agreement about what the new CRM should mean.</p></div>
